<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="52df9849" lines="49-67">
<description>
Complete each partially hidden simple foreground shape by recoloring the occluding pixels in its footprint.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background and several solid foreground shapes.
- Some shapes are partially hidden by other foreground colors, but their full
  footprint is still indicated by a foreground-filled bbox or by a foreground-
  filled 45-degree diagonal line.

Rule
- For each foreground color, complete its full rectangular bbox when that bbox
  contains no background and includes other colors.
- Otherwise, complete its 45-degree diagonal line when all visible pixels lie
  on that line and the hidden line cells are foreground, not background.
- Recolor only those hidden footprint pixels; leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The occluded cells are exactly the non-background cells inside a simple
  shape's implied footprint, so completing shapes is a local recoloring problem.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="5623160b" lines="44-59">
<description>
Push every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.
- Every other foreground 4-connected object touches the marker pixels on exactly one cardinal side.

Rule:
- Clear each non-background, non-maroon object from its original position.
- Translate it in the direction away from its maroon contact until the object reaches the grid boundary.
- Keep the maroon marker pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
- The maroon contact side is a launcher: it determines a unique outward ray for the whole object, and the edge of the grid determines the translation distance.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="bae5c565" lines="10-28">
<description>
Erase the top palette row and redraw it below as a widening wedge centered on its missing cell.

Input structure
- The top row is a palette row with exactly one background gap.
- Below it, the only foreground is one vertical marker bar of a single color.
- The marker bar is either in the gap column or immediately beside it.

Rule
- Clear the grid to background.
- Starting at the top of the marker bar, each next row copies one more
  column of the top-row palette on both sides of the gap.
- Fill the gap column with the marker color, and keep the vertical marker bar.

Key insight
- The lone background cell in the top row is the center of the expanding
  copy; the lower vertical bar provides the center color and the row where
  the expansion begins.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="2ba387bc" lines="6-21">
<description>
Sort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.
- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.
- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.

Rule:
- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.
- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.
- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).
- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.

Key insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="b745798f" lines="18-33">
<description>
Expand each 3-pixel L marker into a same-color corner L on an empty background.

Input structure
- The grid is an odd square with one dominant background color.
- Every foreground object is a monochromatic 4-connected 3-pixel L inside a
  2x2 box.

Rule
- Clear the grid to the background.
- For each marker, map its L orientation to the corresponding outer corner
  and draw that corner L in the marker color with arm length half the grid.

Key insight
- The small marker is a miniature corner; its orientation, not its position,
  chooses which outer corner receives the expanded L.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="22425bda" lines="58-76">
<description>
List the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.

Input structure
The background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part
of one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.
Higher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.

Rule
Recover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where
multiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line
through that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects
those above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that
geometrically cross.

Key insight
The visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer
is the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the
bottom layer to the top layer.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="f0f8a26d" lines="30-44">
<description>
Rotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.

Input structure
The background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.
Each 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical
bar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.

Rule
Clear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,
and color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.

Key insight
The bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output
placement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="13e47133" lines="56-69">
<description>
Fill partitioned regions with concentric colored bands derived from marker pixels.

Input structure:
- Grid partitioned into regions by wall-colored lines.
- Each region has a dominant base color and a few isolated marker pixels of other colors at varying depths from the region boundary.

Rule:
- For each region, read marker colors ordered by their Chebyshev distance from the boundary to build a color palette.
- If no marker sits on the boundary itself, the base color leads the palette.
- Paint every cell with palette[distance mod palette_length], producing repeating concentric rectangular frames that radiate inward.

Key insight: Each marker's depth from the boundary defines its position in the cyclic palette; the pattern then tiles inward automatically.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="a6953f00" lines="45-63">
<description>
Extract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.

Input structure
- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops
  touching the top edge and a grid corner.
- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.
- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.

Rule
- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to
  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.
- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of
  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.

Key insight
- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information
  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="50c07299" lines="8-21">
<description>
Move the diagonal foreground segment to the next longer run along the same up-right diagonal.

Input structure
The grid has one background color and one foreground color. The foreground pixels form a
contiguous one-pixel-wide segment on a single anti-diagonal.

Rule
Erase the current segment. If its columns are c0..c1 and its length is L, draw the same
color on columns c1+1..c1+L+1 along the same anti-diagonal.

Key insight
The segment advances past its current upper-right end by one whole segment position, and
the next segment is one pixel longer than the current one.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="a25697e4" lines="9-23">
<description>
Dock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.
- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.

Rule:
- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.
- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.
- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.

Key insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="9caf5b84" lines="28-45">
<description>
Recolor every pixel whose color is not one of the two most frequent input colors to orange.

Input structure:
- The grid contains at least three colors.
- Orange is absent from the input and is used only as the output replacement color.
- Exactly two colors occupy the top two frequency ranks; they may tie with each
  other, but no third color ties them at the cutoff.

Rule:
- Preserve every pixel whose color is one of the two most frequent input colors.
- Replace every other pixel with orange.

Key insight:
- The visible distinction is not the numeric identity of the colors but their
  population: the two majority colors remain, while all minority colors merge
  into the new orange color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="f28a3cbb" lines="42-50">
<description>
Compress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.
Input structure
- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.
- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.
Rule
- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.
Key insight
- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.

